> Its that type of play which multiplayers consider unfair.

I think you may be painting with too broad a brush here. If you mean "the 
battle.net crowd doesn't like playing on multiplayer maps unless they are 
perfectly symmetrical and predictable" then I wouldn't disagree with that 
statement, but I wouldn't claim that all people who play multiplayer games 
demand that kind of symmetry. I love playing a randomly generated map in a 
multiplayer game and discovering through the fog of war the fortunes of war. 
This is one of the better features, imo, of Dominions, where that sort of 
thing happens all the time with where your sides wind up getting placed on 
some of their unusual maps. But I *like* when that happens and it's one of 
the reasons that I don't play on the "hex" map, which is an extreme example 
of symmetry.

> understandably so. They prefer to win a game based on their own strategy
> vs the strategy of the other player. They dont want a game where the
> loser can claim he lost not to you but instead to randoms.

This is the old argument LShaping used to make, that there is nothing 
strategic about handling random events. I thought that idea had been pretty 
much discredited (along with LShaping himself, but that's a whole 'nother 
fun thread). Tell it to the great generals throughout history who've shown 
their ability to prevail precisely because of their strategic handling of 
the randomness of terrain and other factors thrown at them in the 
unforgiving unpredictability of human battle.

Maybe your point here is that there are enough battle.net type players who 
don't care about that kind of strategy and will whine incessantly for their 
perfect symmetry so much so that it will force developers to strive for it, 
resulting in diminished games of boring predictability and symmetry. If so, 
I agree that this is a risk, but I hope more game designers stand by their 
judgment and design principles. I think it's a lot more fun to make my way 
through the less predictable games offered by a random generator and it's 
one of the reasons that a random map generator was the single most requested 
feature for AoW (whatever you think of how that game handled its other 
challenges).

> NO Im saying that I dont mind games where there are such extreme randoms
> that its possible to end up in a completely unplayable position which
> must be tossed out. Such as ending up on an island with no way to expand.
> Such extreme variables allow for extremely unique sessions. All efforts
> to smooth out impossible situations also gets rid of interesting game
> variants.

Agreed! This is one of the great features of those games that offered random 
map generators (MoM, Imperialism II), and a lot of what made them fun. 
Imperialism II had a great UI for generating and then pre-approving the 
generated maps (multiplayer game).

> A rock-paper-scissors balance is still a balance. One
