Yeah, it is hard to do. That's a point I make below, that it's one of the 
reasons why sticking with SP is a bit of a cop-out if the reason MP is 
avoided is to avoid having to do it. You still have to balance, whether it's 
for SP or MP.

> But if by balanced sides you mean chess-balance appropriate for
> tournament ladders then, no I have no interest in that.\

Agreed, either do I. That's not what's required, for the same reason that 
it's not required when I play vs. the computer.

> You lost me here. I dont see your point. Any game which offers single-
> killer strategys is a crappy game in anyones book isnt it? I dont see
> where having all options equal lead to killer strategy, especialy in a
> multiplayer game.

Let me try and rephrase, because I don't think I expressed myself well. What 
I'm saying in this comment is that I often hear the complaint that adding MP 
is a lazy way of avoiding having to add a good AI. On a related topic 
(granted, not really "opposite"), I think avoiding MP is a lazy way of 
avoiding having to balance. I'd hate to think that a developer decided not 
to add MP because they would then have to ensure a balance that they didn't 
have to worry about for SP games. But I bet it happens. As far as I know, no 
game AI has ever posted a message on Usenet complaining about getting 
bitchslapped because your side started on a river.

